    "rawOutput": "Looking at this problem, I need to find a set B that minimizes |B|²/v where v is the largest integer such that every integer in {1, 2, ..., v} appears as a pairwise difference.\n\nThis is related to the concept of difference sets and Sidon sets. I want to maximize the coverage v while keeping the set size |B| as small as possible.\n\nLet me think about this systematically:\n\n1. For a set of size n, there are at most n(n-1)/2 distinct pairwise differences\n2. To get good coverage, I want the differences to be as dense as possible in the range [1, v]\n3. Known constructions like Singer difference sets or constructions based on quadratic residues can be helpful\n\nLet me try a construction based on powers and careful spacing. I'll start with a relatively small set and try to maximize coverage:\n\nStarting with 0 (automatically included), I'll add elements that create many useful differences while avoiding too much overlap.\n\nAfter analyzing patterns and trying different approaches, here's a set that should give good performance:\n\n```json\n{ \"set\": [0, 1, 3, 6, 13, 20, 27, 39, 49, 56, 61, 69, 73, 84, 89, 97, 114, 122, 139, 142, 152, 175, 177, 188, 204, 218, 227, 240, 253, 259, 278, 296] }\n```\n\nThis construction attempts to balance set size with coverage by:\n- Including 0 and 1 for basic differences\n- Using a mix of arithmetic progressions and carefully chosen gaps\n- Ensuring good coverage of small differences while extending to larger ones\n- Avoiding too much redundancy in the difference structure\n\nThe set has 32 elements and should provide substantial contiguous coverage, giving a reasonable |B|²/v ratio."
